NAME
    srv - manage the portable local lifecycle of a service

SYNOPSIS
    srv start <service>
    srv stop [-f] <service>

DESCRIPTION
    srv provides the portable local service lifecycle owned by the technical m
    layer. The service process remains a foreground program from its own point of
    view; srv performs the surrounding background lifecycle management.

    For a provider-backed service, <service> is a facility identity. srv resolves
    the configured system facility default to one installed concrete provider,
    validates that concrete's materialized facility-service start realization, and
    launches the exact package command belonging to that concrete. Consumer package
    bindings are not consulted. The normal package launcher remains responsible for
    package HOME, environment, and dependency preparation.

    During migration, when no system facility default applies (or a legacy service
    name is outside facility-name grammar), srv retains the historical
    <service>-start PATH lookup as a compatibility path. A configured facility
    default that cannot resolve, or whose selected concrete has no valid service
    realization, fails instead of falling back to PATH.

COMMANDS
    start <service>
        Start the service if it is not already running under valid managed runtime
        state. Standard output and standard error from the service are appended to
        its managed service log.

    stop [-f] <service>
        Stop a managed running service with SIGTERM and wait a finite period for
        clean termination. If no active managed service remains, stop succeeds
        idempotently.

OPTIONS
    -f
        Apply only to stop. Bypass the normal caller-ownership check and explicitly
        request stopping the managed service. This is a lifecycle override, not an
        authentication or security boundary.

STATE
    srv resolves its user-scoped technical run and log roots through state-path:

        state-path user sys srv run
        state-path user sys srv log

    Runtime metadata below those roots is private to srv. Provider-backed running
    instances persist the selected concrete provider identity together with PID,
    caller-owner and exact command identity. Later facility-default changes do not
    retarget the running instance; stop operates on recorded runtime state and does
    not resolve another provider.

    Legacy runtime records without provider metadata remain readable during the
    migration compatibility period.

READINESS
    Successful start establishes that the launched process survived the initial
    launch check. It does not establish application-specific readiness or health.

EXIT STATUS
    0     The requested lifecycle operation completed successfully. This includes
          idempotent stop cases where the service is not running.
    1     A lifecycle operation failed.
    2     The invocation or service name is invalid.
    126   The legacy compatibility <service>-start target is not executable.
    127   No provider-backed default applies and the legacy compatibility
          <service>-start target cannot be resolved.

EXAMPLES
    Start a service named web:

        srv start web

    Stop it normally:

        srv stop web

    Explicitly stop it despite caller-ownership metadata:

        srv stop -f web

